The list is walked until the last node is found. Its `prev` pointer gives the node that will come before the new node. Then the four links around that position are updated.

This changes the existing list directly. For a one-node list, the new node becomes the head.

```cpp
/**
class ListNode
{
 * Definition for doubly-linked list.
 *  public:
 *      int data;
 *      ListNode *prev;
 *      ListNode *next;
 *      ListNode() : data(0), prev(nullptr), next(nullptr) {}
 *      ListNode(int x) : data(x), prev(nullptr), next(nullptr) {}
 *      ListNode(int x, ListNode *prev, ListNode *next) : data(x), prev(prev), next(next) {}
};
*/

class Solution {
public:
    ListNode* insertBeforeTail(ListNode* head, int X) {
        if (head == nullptr) {
            return head;
        }

        if (head->next == nullptr) {
            ListNode* newNode = new ListNode(X);
            newNode->next = head;
            head->prev = newNode;
            return newNode;
        }

        ListNode* tail = head;
        while (tail->next != nullptr) {
            tail = tail->next;
        }

        ListNode* beforeTail = tail->prev;
        ListNode* newNode = new ListNode(X);

        beforeTail->next = newNode;
        newNode->prev = beforeTail;
        newNode->next = tail;
        tail->prev = newNode;

        return head;
    }
};
```

Time complexity: `O(n)` because the tail may be at the end of the list.
Space complexity: `O(1)` extra space, not counting the new node.

I first move to the tail instead of trying to track the previous node separately. In a doubly linked list, `tail.prev` already points to the node before it.

For example, with `1 <-> 3`, inserting `2` changes the links to `1 <-> 2 <-> 3`. The original nodes stay in the list, and only one new node is created.

