Only the links touching the tail and its old previous node need to change. The new node is connected on both sides first, then the neighboring nodes are updated.

This keeps the head unchanged whenever the list has at least two nodes. If the input has one node, that node is the tail, so the inserted node becomes the new head.

```java
/*
// Definition for a Node.
class ListNode {
    public int data;
    public ListNode prev;
    public ListNode next;
    public ListNode();
    public ListNode(int data);
    public ListNode(int data, ListNode prev, ListNode next);
};
*/

class Solution {
    public ListNode insertBeforeTail(ListNode head, int X) {
        if (head == null) {
            return head;
        }

        if (head.next == null) {
            ListNode inserted = new ListNode(X, null, head);
            head.prev = inserted;
            return inserted;
        }

        ListNode current = head;
        while (current.next != null) {
            current = current.next;
        }

        ListNode oldPrevious = current.prev;
        ListNode inserted = new ListNode(X, oldPrevious, current);

        oldPrevious.next = inserted;
        current.prev = inserted;

        return head;
    }
}
```

Time complexity: `O(n)` to reach the tail.
Space complexity: `O(1)` extra space, excluding the node that must be added.

This version counts the nodes first, then walks to the node just before the tail. It makes the special one-node case clear and keeps the pointer changes in one place.

The list is modified in place. A list with two nodes is also handled normally: the new node is placed between them.

```go
/** Definition for a Node.
type ListNode struct {
    Data int
    Next *ListNode
    Prev *ListNode
}
*/

func insertBeforeTail(head *ListNode, data int) *ListNode {
    if head == nil {
        return head
    }

    if head.Next == nil {
        newNode := &ListNode{Data: data, Next: head}
        head.Prev = newNode
        return newNode
    }

    length := 0
    current := head
    for current != nil {
        length++
        current = current.Next
    }

    beforeTail := head
    for position := 1; position < length-1; position++ {
        beforeTail = beforeTail.Next
    }

    tail := beforeTail.Next
    newNode := &ListNode{
        Data: data,
        Prev: beforeTail,
        Next: tail,
    }

    beforeTail.Next = newNode
    tail.Prev = newNode

    return head
}
```

Time complexity: `O(n)`, including the count and second walk.
Space complexity: `O(1)` extra space, apart from the inserted node.
